"Shows semi-autonomous governance circles, their domains and the leader-delegate links that connect related circles.\n\nUse when: Explain how a sociocratic organization distributes policy authority. Check that local circles remain connected through double linking.\n\nAvoid when: The organization uses ordinary management reporting without circle governance.\n\nVisual grammar: Nested or linked circle regions identify aims and authority domains. Leader and delegate markers identify distinct linking roles. Explicit double links show membership connecting parent and focused circles.\n\nBuild: Define each circle's aim, domain and membership. Arrange the scope hierarchy without implying unilateral managerial control. Mark leader and delegate participation in both connected circles.\n\nCheck: Each circle's policy authority has a stated domain. Double links identify actual roles and memberships. Circle structure agrees with the organization's consent-based governance rules.\n\nPitfalls: Drawing ordinary departments as circles without changing their governance meaning. Omitting the delegate and leaving a one-way reporting structure.\n\nExample: A food cooperative separates delivery and store circles, each linked to the general circle through its leader and elected delegate.\n\nAlternatives: Organization chart, RACI matrix."
